window.STORIES = [{"id":"01-a-day-with-my-family","title":"A Day with My Family","words":1574,"paragraphs":["My name is Mai, and I live with my family in a small house near the center of the city. There are five people in my family: my father, my mother, my younger brother Nam, my grandmother, and me. We also have a fat orange cat called Bun. Our house is not big, but it is warm and full of life. I want to tell you about a normal day in our home, because I think normal days are the most important days of all.","Every morning, my grandmother is the first person to wake up. She gets up at five o'clock, before the sun rises. She says that old people do not need much sleep. She goes to the kitchen, boils some water, and makes a pot of green tea. Then she sits by the window and watches the street. She likes to see the people who walk past our house: the woman who sells bread, the man who rides his bike to the market, and the children in their school uniforms. When I ask her why she gets up so early, she smiles and says, \"The morning is the best part of the day. The air is fresh, and the world is quiet.\"","My mother wakes up at half past five. She is a nurse at the hospital near our house, so she has to start work early. Before she leaves, she cooks breakfast for all of us. On most days, we eat rice soup or noodles. Sometimes she makes eggs and bread, because my brother loves them. My mother is a very busy woman, but she never forgets to cook for her family. She always says, \"A good day starts with a good breakfast.\"","My father gets up at six. He works in an office in the city. He is a quiet man who does not talk much in the morning. He drinks a cup of black coffee, reads the news on his phone, and asks us about our plans for the day. Then he takes my brother to school on his motorbike. My brother is ten years old, and he is in the fourth grade. He is a funny and lively boy, but he is not good at waking up. Every morning, my mother has to call his name three or four times. \"Nam! Get up! You will be late!\" And every morning, he says, \"Five more minutes, Mom!\"","I am twenty years old, and I am a student at a university. My classes usually start at eight o'clock, so I leave the house at half past seven. I go to school by bus. The bus is often crowded, but I do not mind. I use the time to read a book or listen to music. Sometimes I look out of the window and think about my day.","At university, I study business. I like my classes, and I have many good friends there. My best friend is Lan. We have been friends since we were in high school. She is kind, clever, and very funny. We often have lunch together in the school cafeteria. The food there is cheap and quite good. After lunch, we sit under a big tree in the garden and talk about our classes, our families, and our dreams for the future. Lan wants to be a teacher. I want to open my own small coffee shop one day.","While I am at school, my grandmother stays at home. She is seventy-five years old, but she is still very active. She cleans the house, waters the plants, and feeds the cat. She also goes to the market every morning to buy fresh vegetables, meat, and fish. Everyone at the market knows her. They call her \"Grandma Hoa.\" She likes to talk with the sellers and ask about their families. When she comes home, she often brings a small gift for us, like a bag of sweet fruit or some cakes.","In the afternoon, my grandmother takes a short nap. Then she listens to the radio or watches old movies on television. Sometimes her friends from the neighborhood come to visit. They sit in our living room, drink tea, eat sunflower seeds, and talk for hours. I love to hear them laugh. They talk about the past, about their children and grandchildren, and about the changes in our city. \"When I was young,\" my grandmother often says, \"there were no tall buildings here. There were only small houses, rice fields, and trees.\"","My brother comes home from school at four o'clock. He is always hungry when he gets home. He throws his school bag on the floor and runs to the kitchen to look for something to eat. My grandmother usually gives him a glass of milk and a piece of cake. Then she tells him to do his homework. My brother does not like homework. He would rather play games on his computer or play football with his friends in the street. But my grandmother is strict. \"Homework first, then games,\" she says. And my brother always listens to her, because he loves her very much.","I come home at about five o'clock. If I have time, I help my brother with his math homework. He is good at English, but math is difficult for him. I try to explain things slowly and clearly. Sometimes he understands quickly, and sometimes he gets angry and says, \"This is too hard!\" Then I tell him, \"Don't worry. Let's try again together.\" In the end, he always finishes his homework, and he is proud of himself.","My mother gets home at six. She is tired after a long day at the hospital, but she still smiles when she sees us. She takes a shower, changes her clothes, and then starts to cook dinner. My grandmother and I help her in the kitchen. My grandmother washes the vegetables, and I cut the meat. My mother is in charge of the cooking. She is an excellent cook. Her fish soup is the best in the world, I think.","My father comes home at about half past six. He usually brings some fruit or a newspaper. He sits in the living room and talks with my brother about his day at school. Sometimes they play a game of chess. My father is very good at chess, but sometimes he lets my brother win. My brother does not know this, and he is always very happy when he wins.","At seven o'clock, we have dinner together. This is my favorite time of the day. We sit around a round table in the kitchen. There is always rice, a soup, some vegetables, and a meat or fish dish. Before we eat, we wait for my grandmother to pick up her chopsticks first. This is a family custom, and it shows our respect for her. During dinner, we talk about everything. My father talks about his work. My mother tells us about her patients. My brother talks about his friends and his teacher. I tell them about my classes and about Lan. My grandmother listens to everyone and gives us advice. We laugh a lot at the dinner table.","After dinner, my brother and I wash the dishes. We do not always enjoy this job, but it is our duty. My brother washes, and I dry. Sometimes we play music on my phone and sing while we work. My parents sit in the living room and watch the news on television. My grandmother cuts some fruit for dessert. Then we all sit together, eat fruit, and talk a little more.","At nine o'clock, my brother goes to bed. He brushes his teeth, puts on his pajamas, and asks my grandmother to tell him a story. My grandmother knows many old stories about kings, dragons, and clever animals. My brother has heard most of them many times, but he still loves to hear them again. He usually falls asleep before the story ends.","I study in my room until about eleven o'clock. My room is small, but it is my favorite place in the house. There is a desk by the window, a bookshelf full of books, and a soft bed with a blue blanket. On the wall, there are photos of my family and friends. When I feel tired, I stop studying and look at the photos. They make me smile.","Before I go to sleep, I often think about my day. My family is not rich, and our life is simple. We do not have a big car or a beautiful garden. But we have each other. We eat together, laugh together, and help each other every day. When I am sad, my mother hugs me. When I have a problem, my father listens to me. When I am tired, my grandmother makes me a cup of hot tea. When I feel lonely, my little brother makes me laugh.","Some of my friends want to move to another country in the future. They dream about big cities, good jobs, and a lot of money. I understand them, and I wish them luck. But I know that I want to stay close to my family. For me, happiness is not about money. Happiness is a warm meal with the people you love, a cup of tea in the morning, and a quiet house at night. Happiness is a normal day with my family."]},{"id":"02-going-to-the-market","title":"Going to the Market","words":1470,"paragraphs":["Every Saturday morning, I go to the local market with my grandmother. It is a small tradition between the two of us, and I enjoy it very much. During the week, I am busy with school and work, so I do not have much time to spend with her. But Saturday morning belongs to us. We wake up early, put on comfortable clothes, take two big shopping bags, and walk to the market together.","The market is about ten minutes from our house. We walk along a narrow street with small shops on both sides. There is a bakery, a hair salon, a shop that sells phones, and a small cafe where old men sit and drink coffee every morning. My grandmother says hello to everyone we pass. She has lived in this neighborhood for more than forty years, so she knows almost everybody.","When we arrive at the market, it is already busy and noisy. People are walking in every direction. Sellers are calling out to customers. Motorbikes are trying to get through the crowd. The air is full of different smells: fresh fish, ripe fruit, cooked meat, flowers, and spices. For some people, the market is too crowded and too loud. But for me, it is one of the most interesting places in the city. It is full of life.","My grandmother always has a plan. Before we leave home, she writes a shopping list on a small piece of paper. She keeps the list in her pocket, and she checks it many times while we shop. Today, the list says: rice, eggs, pork, fish, tomatoes, green vegetables, onions, garlic, bananas, and a chicken for Sunday dinner.","We start with the vegetables. There are many vegetable sellers at the market, but my grandmother always goes to the same woman. Her name is Mrs. Lan, and she has sold vegetables at this market for twenty years. Her vegetables come from her own small farm outside the city. They are not the cheapest, but they are always fresh and clean.","\"Good morning, Grandma Hoa!\" Mrs. Lan says with a big smile. \"And good morning to you too, Mai. You are getting taller every week!\"","My grandmother looks at the vegetables carefully. She picks up a bunch of green leaves, smells them, and checks the color. Then she looks at the tomatoes. She chooses the red, round ones and puts them in a small plastic bag. \"How much are the tomatoes today?\" she asks.","\"Twenty thousand for a kilo,\" Mrs. Lan answers.","\"Twenty thousand? Last week, they were only fifteen thousand!\" my grandmother says.","Mrs. Lan laughs. \"The weather has been very hot this month, and there are not many tomatoes. Everything is more expensive now. But for you, I can make it eighteen thousand.\"","My grandmother smiles and nods. This is a game they play every week. My grandmother always asks for a lower price, and Mrs. Lan always gives her a small discount. In the end, they are both happy. Mrs. Lan also puts a few extra green onions and some herbs into our bag for free. \"For your soup,\" she says.","Next, we go to the meat section. This part of the market is not my favorite. It is hot and wet, and there are many flies. But my grandmother does not care. She walks straight to a tall man with a big knife. He is the pork seller, and his name is Mr. Binh. He cuts the meat very quickly and very well.","\"I need half a kilo of pork, please,\" my grandmother says. \"Not too fat. It is for my grandson. He doesn't like fat.\"","Mr. Binh chooses a nice piece of meat, cuts it, and puts it on the scale. \"That is exactly half a kilo,\" he says. My grandmother watches the scale carefully. She trusts Mr. Binh, but she always checks. She says that you must always pay attention when you spend money.","After the pork, we buy a chicken. My grandmother wants a chicken from the countryside, not a chicken from a big farm. She says that countryside chickens have more flavor, because they walk around freely and eat natural food. They are more expensive, but she thinks they are worth the money. We will cook the chicken with ginger and rice tomorrow, when my uncle and his family come to visit.","Then we go to the fish section. The fish sellers keep the fish in big plastic boxes full of water. Some of the fish are still alive and moving. My grandmother points at one of them. \"That one, please,\" she says. The fish seller catches it with a net, cleans it, and cuts it into pieces. My grandmother will use it to make sour fish soup, which is my father's favorite dish.","By now, our bags are getting heavy. I carry the heavier bag, and my grandmother carries the lighter one. She says she can carry both, but I don't let her. She is seventy-five years old, and I want to take care of her, just like she has always taken care of me.","We still need eggs, rice, and fruit. The egg seller is a young woman with a baby on her back. She sells chicken eggs and duck eggs. My grandmother buys ten chicken eggs. The young woman puts them in a paper bag very carefully, so they don't break. The baby looks at me with big black eyes, and I wave at her. She smiles and hides her face behind her mother's shoulder.","For the rice, we go to an old man who has a small shop at the edge of the market. He sells many different kinds of rice in big bags: white rice, brown rice, sticky rice, and black rice. My grandmother always buys five kilos of white rice. The old man weighs the rice and puts it in a strong bag. Rice is very heavy, so he offers to bring it to our house later on his bike. My grandmother thanks him and pays him.","Finally, we go to the fruit section. This is my favorite part of the market. There are so many colors! There are yellow bananas, orange mangoes, green watermelons, purple grapes, red apples, and many fruits that I don't know the names of in English. My grandmother buys a bunch of bananas and a big papaya. I ask her if we can also buy some mangoes, and she says yes. She knows that I love mangoes more than any other fruit.","While we are walking to the exit, we see a woman selling hot sticky rice with beans. My grandmother looks at me and smiles. \"Are you hungry?\" she asks. I say yes, of course. She buys two small packs of sticky rice, wrapped in banana leaves. We sit on two little plastic chairs next to the seller and eat our breakfast. The sticky rice is warm, soft, and a little sweet. It is simple food, but it tastes wonderful.","While we eat, my grandmother tells me about the market in the past. \"When I was a young woman,\" she says, \"this market was much smaller. There was no roof. When it rained, we got wet. There were no plastic bags. People brought their own baskets. And everything was very cheap. One egg cost less than a cup of tea!\"","I like to hear her stories. They help me understand how much our city has changed. Today, there are big supermarkets everywhere. Many young people prefer to shop at supermarkets, because they are clean, cool, and comfortable. You can find everything in one place, and you don't need to ask about prices. Some people even buy their food online and never go to the market at all.","I asked my grandmother once why she still goes to the market instead of the supermarket. She thought for a moment and then said, \"At the supermarket, you buy food. At the market, you meet people. Mrs. Lan, Mr. Binh, the egg seller, the old rice man, they are not just sellers. They are my friends. I know their children's names. When I was sick last year, Mrs. Lan came to my house with a bag of vegetables and a pot of soup. A supermarket cannot do that.\"","I think about her words as we walk home with our heavy bags. The sun is higher now, and the street is getting hot. My arms are tired, but I feel happy. I know that one day my grandmother will be too old to come to the market. When that day comes, I will continue our Saturday tradition. I will walk to the market, say hello to Mrs. Lan, buy the freshest vegetables, and ask for a small discount, just like my grandmother taught me."]},{"id":"03-cooking-dinner-together","title":"Cooking Dinner Together","words":1629,"paragraphs":["Last Friday, my friends and I decided to cook dinner together at my apartment. There were four of us: me, my roommate Linh, our friend Tuan, and his girlfriend Anna. Anna is from Australia, and she has lived in Vietnam for two years. She teaches English at a language school, and she loves Vietnamese food. For a long time, she had asked us to teach her how to cook some of our favorite dishes. So we finally made a plan.","We met at the supermarket at five o'clock in the afternoon. Before we went, we wrote a menu and a shopping list. Our menu had four dishes: fresh spring rolls, fried fish with tomato sauce, morning glory with garlic, and a simple chicken soup. For dessert, we wanted to make a sweet mango drink. It was a lot of food for four people, but we were all very hungry, and we wanted to have leftovers for the weekend.","At the supermarket, we took a cart and divided the list. Tuan and Anna went to buy the fish, the chicken, and the shrimp. Linh and I went to the vegetable section. We needed lettuce, herbs, cucumbers, carrots, tomatoes, garlic, onions, morning glory, limes, and chili. We also bought rice paper for the spring rolls, rice noodles, a bottle of fish sauce, and a bag of sugar. Anna also wanted some cold drinks, so we bought a few bottles of juice and some soda.","When we got to the checkout, the total was a little high. \"This is more expensive than eating at a restaurant,\" Tuan said with a laugh. But Linh said, \"Yes, but cooking together is more fun. And we will have food for three days!\" We divided the bill between the four of us, and it was not so bad.","My apartment is on the sixth floor of an old building. There is no elevator, so we had to carry all the heavy bags up the stairs. By the time we reached my door, we were all tired and sweaty. Anna laughed and said, \"Well, now we really need a good dinner!\"","My kitchen is very small. There is only one small table, a sink, a fridge, a stove with two burners, and a rice cooker. Four people in that kitchen is a little crowded. So we decided to work in two teams. Linh and Anna would prepare the spring rolls on the dining table in the living room. Tuan and I would cook the hot dishes in the kitchen.","First, we washed all the vegetables. In Vietnam, we always wash vegetables very carefully, often two or three times. Anna was surprised. \"In Australia, we usually just wash them once,\" she said. Linh explained that the vegetables at the market sometimes have a lot of dirt on them, so it is important to clean them well. After we washed them, we put the herbs and lettuce in a big basket to dry.","Then Tuan started to cook the rice. He put three cups of rice into the pot of the rice cooker, washed it with water two times, and then added fresh water. \"How do you know how much water to use?\" Anna asked. Tuan smiled and showed her an old trick. He put his finger into the pot, touching the top of the rice. \"The water should come up to the first line of your finger,\" he said. \"My mother taught me this. It works every time.\" Anna tried it too, and she laughed. \"That's amazing. No need to measure anything!\"","While the rice was cooking, Linh showed Anna how to make spring rolls. First, she boiled the shrimp for three minutes and cut each one in half. She also boiled some pork and cut it into thin slices. Then she cooked the rice noodles in hot water. When everything was ready, she put it all on big plates on the table, next to the herbs, lettuce, and cucumber.","\"Now comes the difficult part,\" Linh said. She took a piece of dry rice paper and put it into a bowl of warm water for just one second. Then she put it on a plate. The rice paper became soft very quickly. She put some lettuce, noodles, herbs, a few slices of pork, and two halves of a shrimp on the paper. Then she folded the sides and rolled it tightly. In less than a minute, she had a perfect spring roll.","Anna tried to make one. Her first roll was a disaster. She left the rice paper in the water for too long, so it became too soft and stuck to the plate. When she tried to roll it, the paper broke, and the noodles fell out. Everyone laughed, including Anna. \"This looks easy when you do it!\" she said to Linh. \"Don't worry,\" Linh answered. \"My first spring rolls were terrible too. Try again.\"","Anna tried again, and again, and again. By her fifth roll, she was much better. By her tenth roll, it looked almost perfect. She was very proud of herself and took a photo of it with her phone. \"I'm going to send this to my mother,\" she said. \"She will not believe that I made it.\"","In the kitchen, Tuan and I were working on the fish. We bought a big fish, which the seller had already cleaned for us. I cut it into four large pieces and added a little salt. Then I heated some oil in a pan. When the oil was very hot, I carefully put the fish into the pan. The oil made a loud noise, and some of it jumped out of the pan. \"Be careful!\" Tuan said. I stepped back quickly. Hot oil can give you a bad burn.","I fried the fish for about five minutes on each side, until it was golden and crispy. Meanwhile, Tuan made the tomato sauce. He cut the tomatoes into small pieces and cooked them in another pan with some oil, garlic, and onions. Then he added a little fish sauce, sugar, and water. He let the sauce cook slowly until it was thick and red. When the fish was ready, we put it on a big plate and poured the hot tomato sauce over it. It smelled delicious.","Next was the morning glory. This is a very common vegetable in Vietnam, and almost every family eats it. It is cheap, healthy, and very easy to cook. Tuan heated some oil in the pan and added a lot of chopped garlic. When the garlic turned light brown, he added the morning glory and cooked it on high heat for only two minutes, stirring all the time. Then he added a little salt and fish sauce. \"The secret is to cook it fast,\" he said. \"If you cook it too long, it becomes soft and dark.\"","The last hot dish was the chicken soup. I had already put the chicken in a big pot of water earlier, with some onions, ginger, and salt. It cooked slowly for about forty minutes while we made the other dishes. Now the soup was clear, and it smelled wonderful. I took the chicken out, cut it into pieces, and put it back into the soup with some green onions and pepper.","Meanwhile, Anna and Linh made the mango drink. They peeled three ripe mangoes, cut them into pieces, and put them in a blender with ice, milk, and a little sugar. Anna tasted it and closed her eyes. \"This is heaven,\" she said. We all laughed.","At about eight o'clock, dinner was finally ready. We put all the dishes on the table in the living room: the spring rolls with their dipping sauce, the fried fish with tomato sauce, the morning glory, the chicken soup, and a big bowl of hot rice. It looked like a real feast. We were very hungry, and the food smelled amazing.","Before we started, Anna took a picture of the table. \"I want to remember this,\" she said. Then we sat on the floor around the table, Vietnamese style, and started to eat. Everyone took food from the dishes in the middle and put it in their own bowl. Anna was already very good at using chopsticks. She tried a little bit of everything.","\"Which dish do you like best?\" Tuan asked her.","Anna thought for a moment. \"It is very hard to choose,\" she said. \"The fish is delicious, and the soup is so comforting. But I think the spring rolls are my favorite. Maybe because I made them myself!\" Everyone laughed again.","We ate, talked, and laughed for a long time. We talked about our childhood, our families, and the food we ate when we were young. Anna told us about barbecues on the beach in Australia. Tuan told us about his grandmother's cooking in the countryside. Linh told a funny story about the first time she tried to cook rice and burned the whole pot.","After dinner, nobody wanted to wash the dishes. So we made a game of it. We played rock, paper, scissors, and the two losers had to wash. Tuan and I lost. While we washed the dishes in the small kitchen, we could hear Linh and Anna laughing in the living room.","Later that night, after Tuan and Anna went home, I thought about our dinner. It was not a special day. It was not a holiday or a birthday. But it was one of the best evenings I have had in a long time. Good food is wonderful, but good food with good friends is even better. We have already decided to cook together again next month. Next time, Anna will teach us how to make an Australian meat pie."]},{"id":"04-my-first-day-at-a-new-job","title":"My First Day at a New Job","words":1611,"paragraphs":["I will never forget my first day at my new job. It was a Monday in early September. The weather was still hot, but the mornings were starting to feel a little cooler. I was twenty-three years old, and I had just finished university. For three months, I had looked for a job. I sent my application to more than thirty companies. Some never answered. Some invited me for an interview but then chose someone else. I was starting to feel hopeless. Then, one afternoon, my phone rang. It was a woman from a small travel company. \"We would like to offer you the job,\" she said. I was so happy that I almost dropped my phone.","The night before my first day, I could not sleep. I was excited, but I was also very nervous. I worried about everything. What should I wear? What if I was late? What if my boss did not like me? What if I made a terrible mistake? I lay in bed and looked at the ceiling for hours. At about two o'clock in the morning, I finally fell asleep.","My alarm rang at six o'clock. I got up immediately, took a quick shower, and put on the clothes I had prepared the night before: a white shirt, dark blue pants, and black shoes. My mother had ironed my shirt for me. She also made me a big breakfast, but I was too nervous to eat much. I only ate a little bread and drank a glass of milk. My mother hugged me at the door. \"Don't worry,\" she said. \"Just be yourself, and be polite. Everything will be fine.\"","The office was in a tall building in the center of the city. I took the bus, and I arrived thirty minutes early. I did not want to go in too early, so I walked around the block two times. Then I bought a coffee from a small shop on the corner and drank it slowly. At ten minutes to eight, I took a deep breath and walked into the building.","The company was on the twelfth floor. When the elevator doors opened, I saw a small, bright office with big windows. There were about fifteen desks, some plants, and a long table in the middle of the room. On the walls, there were beautiful photos of mountains, beaches, and old towns. A young woman at the front desk smiled at me. \"Good morning! You must be the new person,\" she said. \"Welcome! My name is Thu. I'm the receptionist.\"","Thu took me to meet my manager, Mr. Duc. He was a man of about forty-five, with short gray hair and glasses. He had a serious face, and at first, I was a little afraid of him. But when he started to talk, he was very friendly and kind. \"Welcome to our team,\" he said, shaking my hand. \"We are a small company, so everyone here is like family. Don't be afraid to ask questions. Nobody expects you to know everything on your first day.\"","Mr. Duc showed me my desk. It was next to a window, and I could see the river and the bridge from my chair. On the desk, there was a computer, a phone, a notebook, some pens, and a small plant. There was also a card that said, \"Welcome to the team!\" Everyone in the office had signed it. I felt very touched.","Then Mr. Duc introduced me to my colleagues. There was Hung, who planned tours to the mountains in the north. There was Phuong, who worked with hotels and restaurants. There was Kevin, an American man who wrote all the English information on the company website. And there was Ha, a woman about my age who had joined the company six months before me. Mr. Duc told me that Ha would help me during my first few weeks.","Ha was very friendly. She explained how the company worked and what my job would be. My main job was to answer emails and phone calls from customers, help them choose a tour, and book their hotels and tickets. Many of our customers were foreign tourists, so I needed to use English every day. This made me a little nervous, because my English was good, but not perfect.","\"Don't worry,\" Ha said. \"I was nervous too when I started. Just speak slowly and clearly. If you don't understand something, ask the customer to repeat it. Most people are very patient.\"","For the first two hours, Ha showed me how to use the computer system. It was quite complicated. There were many different pages and buttons, and I had to remember a lot of passwords. I wrote everything down in my notebook. Sometimes I felt lost, but Ha was very patient. She explained things two or three times when I did not understand.","At ten o'clock, the phone on my desk rang. Ha looked at me and smiled. \"Do you want to try?\" she asked. My heart started to beat very fast. I picked up the phone. \"Good morning, this is Green Path Travel. How can I help you?\" I said. My voice sounded strange, but I kept going.","The caller was a woman from England. She wanted to book a two-day tour to a famous bay for her family. She spoke very fast, and she had an accent that I was not used to. I asked her to speak more slowly, and she did. I asked her about the dates, the number of people, and the kind of hotel she wanted. I wrote everything down carefully. Ha sat next to me and helped me find the right information on the computer. After about fifteen minutes, everything was ready. The woman thanked me and said, \"You've been very helpful.\" When I put down the phone, my hands were shaking, but I felt very proud.","\"See? You did a great job,\" Ha said.","At twelve o'clock, it was time for lunch. Everyone in the office usually eats together. Some people bring food from home, and some people buy food from the small restaurants near the building. That day, my colleagues took me to a noodle shop on the next street. It was a small, noisy place with plastic chairs and tables, but the food was excellent. We all ordered beef noodle soup. While we ate, my colleagues asked me about my family, my studies, and my hobbies. They also told me funny stories about the company and about some of their strangest customers.","Kevin told us about a customer who once asked him if there were any tigers in the city. Hung told us about a tour group who got lost in the mountains because they did not want to follow the guide. Everyone laughed. I started to feel more relaxed. My colleagues were not scary at all. They were normal, friendly people, just like me.","After lunch, I worked on emails. There were many questions from customers. Some wanted to know about prices. Some wanted to change the dates of their tours. One man wanted to know if he could bring his dog on a boat trip. I did not know the answer to many of the questions, so I asked Ha or Mr. Duc. They always helped me. Slowly, I started to understand how everything worked.","At around three o'clock, I made my first mistake. I sent an email to the wrong customer. It was a list of hotel prices for a family of five, but I sent it to a young couple who had asked about a tour for two people. When I realized what I had done, my face turned red. I felt terrible. I went to Mr. Duc and told him what happened. I was sure that he would be angry.","But Mr. Duc only smiled. \"Everyone makes mistakes,\" he said. \"The important thing is that you told me. Just send the couple a new email and say sorry. Then send the right email to the family.\" I did what he said, and the problem was solved in five minutes. The young couple even wrote back and said, \"No problem! Have a nice day!\" I learned an important lesson that day. It is okay to make mistakes, but you must be honest about them.","At five thirty, it was time to go home. I was very tired. My head was full of new information, new names, and new passwords. But I was also very happy. Before I left, Mr. Duc came to my desk. \"How was your first day?\" he asked. \"It was difficult, but I enjoyed it,\" I answered honestly. He nodded. \"That's a good answer. Tomorrow will be easier. See you in the morning.\"","On the bus home, I looked out of the window at the busy streets and thought about my day. In the morning, I was so nervous that I could not eat. Now, I felt like I belonged somewhere. When I got home, my mother was waiting for me with dinner. \"So?\" she asked. \"How was it?\"","I smiled and said, \"Mom, I think I'm going to like this job.\"","That was three years ago. Today, I am still working at the same company. I have learned a lot, and I have made many good friends. Ha is now one of my best friends. Last month, a new young man joined our team. On his first day, he looked very nervous. I went to his desk, smiled, and said, \"Welcome to the team. Don't be afraid to ask questions. Nobody expects you to know everything on your first day.\""]},{"id":"05-a-visit-to-the-doctor","title":"A Visit to the Doctor","words":1567,"paragraphs":["I am not a person who gets sick very often. I eat well, I exercise three times a week, and I usually sleep seven or eight hours every night. In the last five years, I have only had a few colds. So when I started to feel sick last month, I did not pay much attention. I thought it was nothing serious. That was a mistake.","It started on a Tuesday. I woke up with a sore throat and a slight headache. I thought maybe I had slept with the fan on too high, or maybe I had talked too much the day before. I drank a cup of hot tea with honey and lemon, took a pill for my headache, and went to work as usual. During the day, I felt tired, but I thought it was because I had not slept well.","On Wednesday, I felt worse. My throat hurt more, and I started to cough. My whole body ached, especially my back and my legs. In the afternoon, I felt very cold, even though the office was warm. My colleague, Hanh, looked at me and said, \"Are you okay? Your face is very red.\" She touched my forehead with the back of her hand. \"You're hot! I think you have a fever. You should go home.\"","I did not want to go home. We had a lot of work that week, and I did not want to leave my team. But Hanh insisted. She also told our manager, and he agreed with her. \"Go home and rest,\" he said. \"If you are not better tomorrow, go to the doctor. Your health is more important than work.\" So I took a taxi home, went straight to bed, and slept for three hours.","When I woke up, I felt even worse. I took my temperature with the thermometer that my mother had given me when I moved into my own apartment. It was thirty-nine degrees. I had a high fever. I was alone in my apartment, and I felt weak and a little scared. I called my mother. She lives in another city, about two hours away by bus.","\"You need to see a doctor,\" she said immediately. \"Don't wait. Go tomorrow morning. And drink a lot of water tonight. Eat some rice soup if you can.\"","That night was very long. I could not sleep well. I was hot, then cold, then hot again. I coughed a lot, and my throat felt like it was on fire. I drank glass after glass of water. At about midnight, my neighbor, an old woman who lives across the hall, heard me coughing. She knocked on my door and gave me a bowl of hot rice soup with ginger and green onions. \"Eat this,\" she said. \"It will help you feel better.\" I was very grateful. I did not know her very well before that night, but she was so kind to me.","The next morning, I went to a clinic near my home. It was a small private clinic, and it opened at seven o'clock. I arrived at a quarter past seven, but there were already about ten people in the waiting room. There was an old man with a bad cough, a young mother with a crying baby, a boy with a broken arm, and several other people who looked tired and sick. I went to the front desk and gave my name to the receptionist.","\"Is this your first visit?\" she asked.","\"Yes, it is,\" I answered.","She gave me a form to fill in. I had to write my name, my date of birth, my address, my phone number, and information about my health. There were questions like \"Do you have any allergies?\" and \"Are you taking any medicine at the moment?\" I answered all the questions and gave the form back to her. \"Please take a seat,\" she said. \"The doctor will call your name.\"","I sat in the waiting room for almost an hour. There was a television on the wall, but I did not watch it. I was too tired. I just closed my eyes and waited. Sometimes I coughed, and the old man next to me looked at me with sympathy. \"This weather,\" he said, shaking his head. \"Hot one day, rainy the next. Everybody is getting sick.\"","Finally, a nurse called my name. She took me to a small room where she checked my weight, my height, and my blood pressure. She also took my temperature again. It was still thirty-eight point five degrees. She wrote everything down and told me to wait for the doctor.","A few minutes later, the doctor came in. She was a woman of about fifty, with short black hair and a calm, gentle voice. \"Good morning,\" she said with a smile. \"What seems to be the problem?\"","I told her about my symptoms: the sore throat, the cough, the headache, the fever, and the pain in my body. She listened carefully and asked me some questions. \"When did it start? Do you have a runny nose? Are you coughing anything up? Have you been near anyone who was sick?\" I answered every question as well as I could.","Then she examined me. She looked inside my mouth and my ears with a small light. She felt the sides of my neck. She listened to my chest and my back with her stethoscope. \"Take a deep breath,\" she said. \"Again. Now cough, please.\" She listened for a long time. Then she put her stethoscope away and sat down at her desk.","\"You have a bad throat infection,\" she said. \"Your lungs sound okay, which is good. But the infection is quite serious, and that is why you have a high fever. I will give you some medicine. You need to take it for seven days. It is very important to finish all of it, even if you feel better after three or four days.\"","She wrote a prescription and explained everything to me. \"Take these pills three times a day, after meals. These ones are for your fever and your pain. Take them only when you need them, but not more than four times a day. And this is a syrup for your cough. Take one spoon before bed.\"","\"Can I go to work?\" I asked.","She shook her head. \"No. You need to rest for at least three days. Stay at home, sleep as much as you can, and drink a lot of water. Warm drinks are good for your throat. Eat soft food, like rice soup or noodle soup. Don't drink anything cold, and don't eat spicy food. If your fever is not better in three days, or if you have trouble breathing, come back immediately.\"","I thanked the doctor and went to the front desk to pay. The visit was not cheap, but I had health insurance from my company, so I did not have to pay for everything. Then I went to the pharmacy next door. The pharmacist was a young man who explained the medicine to me again, very clearly. He also gave me some sweets for my throat. \"These will help with the pain,\" he said.","When I got home, I sent a message to my manager and told him what the doctor said. He answered quickly. \"Don't worry about work. Take care of yourself. We will see you next week.\" I also called my mother, and she was very relieved. \"I told you to see a doctor,\" she said. \"You are always too stubborn.\"","For the next three days, I stayed in bed. I slept a lot, took my medicine on time, and drank so much water that I lost count of the glasses. My neighbor came to visit me every day. She brought me rice soup, fruit, and once even a small pot of chicken soup. We talked a lot, and I learned that she had been a teacher for thirty years. She had two sons who lived in other countries, and she often felt lonely. I promised to visit her more often when I was better.","On the fourth day, my fever was gone. On the fifth day, my throat did not hurt anymore. On the seventh day, I finished my medicine, and I felt almost normal again. I went back to work on the following Monday. My colleagues were happy to see me. Hanh gave me a small bag of oranges. \"For your vitamins,\" she said.","This experience taught me a few important lessons. First, when your body tells you something is wrong, you should listen. If I had gone to the doctor on the first day, maybe I would not have been so sick. Second, rest is not lazy. Sometimes, the best thing you can do for yourself and for others is to stay at home and get better. And third, kindness can come from unexpected places. Before I was sick, my neighbor was just an old woman I said hello to in the hall. Now, she is my friend. Every Sunday, I visit her, and we drink tea together. She tells me stories about her students, and I tell her about my work. When I think about that terrible week, I don't remember the fever or the pain so much. I remember her bowl of hot rice soup at midnight."]},{"id":"06-cleaning-the-house-on-sunday","title":"Cleaning the House on Sunday","words":1571,"paragraphs":["In our family, Sunday morning is cleaning time. It has been like this since I was a little girl. During the week, everyone is busy with work and school, so the house slowly becomes messy. There are shoes by the door, books on the table, clothes on the chairs, and dust on every shelf. By Saturday night, our home does not look very nice. So every Sunday, after breakfast, my mother stands in the middle of the living room, claps her hands, and says, \"Okay, everyone! It's time to clean!\"","When I was a child, I hated Sunday cleaning. I wanted to watch cartoons on television or play with my friends outside. I always tried to find an excuse. \"I have a lot of homework,\" I would say. Or, \"My stomach hurts.\" But my mother always knew when I was lying. She would just smile and give me a cloth and a bucket of water. Now that I am older, I understand why she did it. Cleaning together taught us to work as a team and to take care of our home. And, to be honest, I now enjoy it a little.","My mother is the leader of the cleaning team. At the beginning, she writes a list of jobs and gives each person a task. She says that if everybody knows exactly what to do, the work will be faster and nobody will argue. Last Sunday, the list looked like this: Dad cleans the bathroom and the kitchen. Mai cleans the bedrooms and does the laundry. Nam takes out the trash, cleans the windows, and waters the plants. Grandma sorts the old newspapers and folds the clean clothes. Mom sweeps and mops all the floors.","My father always gets the bathroom, and he always complains about it. \"Why is it always me?\" he says. My mother answers, \"Because you are the best at it.\" My father shakes his head, but he goes to the bathroom anyway. He puts on a pair of rubber gloves, takes a brush and a bottle of cleaner, and starts to scrub the toilet, the sink, and the shower. He sings old songs while he works. He is not a very good singer, but his singing makes the work more fun for everybody.","I start with my own bedroom, because it is the messiest room in the house. I am not a very tidy person, I must say. During the week, I put my clothes on my chair instead of in the closet. I leave cups and plates on my desk. There are books and papers everywhere. On Sunday, I have to face the mess. First, I open the window to let fresh air in. Then I pick up all the clothes. Some are clean, so I fold them and put them in the closet. Some are dirty, so I put them in the laundry basket. Next, I take all the cups and plates to the kitchen. Then I organize my desk. I put the books back on the shelf and throw away old papers that I don't need anymore.","After that, I change the sheets on my bed. My mother says we must wash our sheets and pillow covers every week, especially in the hot season, when we sweat a lot at night. I take off the old sheets and put on clean ones. I love the smell of clean sheets. On Sunday night, my bed always feels softer and more comfortable than on any other night.","Then I dust the furniture. I use a soft cloth to clean the top of my desk, my bookshelf, the window, and the lamp. I am always surprised by how much dust there is after only one week. Our house is near a busy road, and dust from the street comes in through the windows every day. When I finish my room, I go to my brother's room. His room is even messier than mine. There are toys, comic books, and socks all over the floor. I find a half-eaten cookie under his bed. \"Nam!\" I shout. \"Come and look at this!\" He runs in, sees the cookie, and laughs. \"Oh, I was looking for that!\" he says. I make him pick up all his toys and socks before I continue.","While I am cleaning the bedrooms, the washing machine is working. I put in the first load of laundry: white shirts, sheets, and towels. Our washing machine is old and very loud. When it spins, the whole bathroom shakes. My father always says that we need a new one, but my mother says that it still works fine, so there is no reason to waste money. When the machine stops, I take the wet clothes out and hang them on the clothesline on our balcony. The sun is strong, so the clothes will be dry in two or three hours. Then I put in the second load: dark clothes, jeans, and socks.","My brother's first job is to take out the trash. In our neighborhood, we must separate our rubbish into different bags. One bag is for food waste, one bag is for plastic and paper that can be recycled, and one bag is for everything else. At first, my brother did not care about this. He put everything in one bag. But last year, his teacher at school talked to the class about the environment and about how much plastic ends up in the ocean. Now, my brother is very serious about recycling. He checks every bag very carefully. Sometimes he even tells my father off. \"Dad! This plastic bottle doesn't go in that bag!\" My father laughs and says, \"Yes, sir!\"","Next, my brother cleans the windows. He uses a spray bottle with water and a little soap, and he wipes the glass with old newspapers. My grandmother taught him this trick. She says newspaper is better than a cloth because it doesn't leave any marks on the glass. My brother likes this job because he can stand on a chair and look outside. Sometimes he stops working to watch the people and motorbikes in the street. My mother has to remind him to keep going.","His last job is to water the plants. We have many plants on our balcony and in front of the house. There are flowers, herbs, a small lemon tree, and some chili plants. My grandmother loves her plants, and she talks to them every morning. She believes that plants grow better when you talk to them kindly. My brother thinks this is funny, but he still says \"good morning\" to the lemon tree when he waters it.","My grandmother's job is easy, because she is old and we don't want her to work too hard. She sits in the living room and sorts the old newspapers and magazines into piles. Some of them she keeps, because there are recipes or interesting stories she wants to read again. The rest we will sell to the man who comes around the neighborhood on his bike every week to buy old paper and bottles. Later, when the clothes are dry, my grandmother folds them. She folds them very neatly, much better than any of us can. Every shirt, every towel, and every pair of pants becomes a perfect square.","My mother does the floors last, because she wants all the dust from the other jobs to fall on the floor first. She starts by sweeping every room with a big broom. Then she fills a bucket with warm water and a little floor cleaner and mops the whole house, starting from the bedrooms and finishing at the front door. While the floors are wet, nobody can walk inside. So we all sit on the balcony together and wait for the floors to dry.","This is my favorite part of Sunday cleaning. We are all a little tired and sweaty, but the hard work is finished. My mother brings out a big plate of cut fruit: watermelon, mango, and pineapple. My father makes a pot of tea. My grandmother tells us about how people cleaned their homes when she was young. \"We had no washing machine,\" she says. \"We washed every piece of clothing by hand, in the river. It took the whole day!\" My brother cannot believe it. \"The whole day? Just for washing?\" he asks. My grandmother nods. \"You children are very lucky,\" she says.","After about half an hour, the floors are dry, and we go back inside. The house looks completely different. Everything is clean, bright, and in its right place. The floors shine, the windows are clear, and the air smells fresh. My mother looks around with a happy face. \"Good job, team,\" she says. \"Now, who wants to help me cook lunch?\"","Sometimes my friends ask me why we don't hire someone to clean our house. Some families in our city do that. But my mother says that cleaning your own home is important. It teaches you responsibility. It helps you appreciate the things you have. And when you clean together, you also spend time together. I think she is right. Some of my happiest memories are from these Sunday mornings: my father singing in the bathroom, my brother talking to the lemon tree, my grandmother's perfect towels, and all of us sitting together on the balcony, eating fruit and waiting for the floors to dry."]},{"id":"07-taking-the-bus-across-the-city","title":"Taking the Bus Across the City","words":1509,"paragraphs":["For many years, I went everywhere on my motorbike. It was fast and easy, and I could go wherever I wanted, whenever I wanted. But last year, my motorbike was stolen from outside a coffee shop. I was very upset. A new motorbike was too expensive for me at that time, so I decided to try the bus for a few months. I thought it would be terrible. I was wrong. Today, I still take the bus every day, even though I have enough money to buy a new motorbike. Let me tell you why.","My first day on the bus was not easy. I did not know which bus to take or where the bus stops were. I asked my neighbor, an old man who takes the bus every day, and he helped me. He showed me an app on my phone that tells you which bus to take, where to wait, and when the next bus will arrive. \"It is very simple,\" he said. \"You type where you are and where you want to go, and the app does the rest.\"","The next morning, I left my house at seven o'clock and walked to the bus stop at the end of my street. It took about five minutes. There were already six or seven people waiting: two students in school uniforms, a woman with a big bag of vegetables, a young man in a suit, and an old woman with a small dog in a basket. Nobody talked. Most people were looking at their phones. I checked the app. My bus, number 32, would arrive in four minutes.","When the bus came, everybody got on through the front door. I was not sure what to do, so I watched the person in front of me. She showed a card to the driver and walked to the back. I did not have a card, so I asked the driver how much the ticket was. \"Seven thousand,\" he said. I gave him the money, and he gave me a small paper ticket. Later, I learned that you can buy a monthly pass that is much cheaper if you take the bus every day.","The bus was quite full, and there were no empty seats. I had to stand and hold onto a bar above my head. At first, I was a little worried. The bus moved slowly through the heavy traffic, and every time it stopped, I almost lost my balance. But after a few stops, I got used to it. I looked around. There were people of all ages: office workers, students, grandmothers, a few tourists with big backpacks. A young woman was reading a book. An old man was sleeping with his head against the window. Two school girls were sharing a pair of headphones and laughing quietly.","My office is on the other side of the city, about twelve kilometers from my home. On my motorbike, the trip took about thirty minutes. On the bus, it took almost an hour, and I had to change buses once. At first, this seemed like a big waste of time. But then I realized something important. On my motorbike, I had to pay attention to the road every second. I had to watch the cars, the other motorbikes, the people crossing the street, the traffic lights. When I got to work, I was always tired and stressed. On the bus, someone else was driving. I could relax.","So I started to use my time on the bus in different ways. On some days, I read a book. In the past year, I have read more books than in the five years before. On other days, I listen to podcasts or learn English with an app on my phone. Sometimes I just look out of the window and watch the city. I have noticed so many things that I never saw before from my motorbike: a beautiful old house with a red door, a small park where old people do exercises every morning, a tiny shop that sells the best bread in the neighborhood, a street full of flower sellers.","I have also met some interesting people on the bus. There is an old woman who takes the same bus as me every morning. She goes to a temple in the city center to pray. At first, we only smiled at each other. Then one day, she offered me a piece of candy, and we started to talk. Now, we chat every morning. She tells me about her grandchildren, and I tell her about my work. Once, when I was sick and did not take the bus for a few days, she asked the driver about me. When I came back, she gave me a small bag of oranges. \"For your health,\" she said.","There is also a young man who plays the guitar at the bus station where I change buses. He is a university student, and he plays music to earn a little extra money. He is very good. Every morning, I put a few coins in his guitar case. Sometimes, if I am early, I stay and listen to a song or two. It is a nice way to start the day.","Of course, the bus is not always pleasant. During rush hour, it can be very crowded, especially in the evening. Sometimes there are so many people that you cannot move at all. In the hot season, even with the air conditioner on, the bus can be uncomfortable. And sometimes, the bus is late. Once, I waited for forty minutes in the rain because there was an accident on the road. I was very late for work, and my clothes were wet. On days like that, I miss my motorbike.","There are also some rules on the bus that not everyone follows. You should give your seat to old people, pregnant women, people with small children, and people with disabilities. Most people do this without being asked. Once, I saw a teenage boy jump up from his seat when an old man got on the bus. The old man thanked him with a big smile. But sometimes, people pretend they are sleeping or busy with their phones so they don't have to stand up. You also should not talk too loudly, eat food with a strong smell, or play music without headphones. Most people are polite, but not everyone.","One day, something happened on the bus that I will never forget. It was a Friday evening, and the bus was very crowded. A small boy, maybe five or six years old, was standing near the door. He was crying. He had got on the bus with his mother, but she did not get on in time, and the doors closed. The boy was alone and very scared. Immediately, several passengers came to help. A woman held his hand and talked to him softly. A man went to tell the driver. The driver stopped the bus at the next stop and called the bus company. In less than fifteen minutes, the boy's mother arrived on a motorbike taxi. She was crying too. She hugged her son tightly and thanked everyone on the bus again and again. When the bus started to move again, all the passengers were smiling. For a moment, we were not strangers. We were a small community.","Taking the bus has also helped me save money. I don't have to pay for gas, parking, or repairs. My monthly bus pass costs less than one tank of gas for a motorbike. In one year, I have saved enough money to take a short trip to the beach with my family. I also feel good that I am helping the environment a little. One bus can carry fifty or sixty people. If all those people drove their own motorbikes, the air in our city would be even dirtier, and the traffic would be even worse.","My friends often ask me why I don't buy a new motorbike. \"The bus is so slow,\" they say. \"And it's so crowded.\" They are right. The bus is slower, and it is often crowded. But I have learned that faster is not always better. On the bus, I have time to read, to think, and to rest. I see my city with new eyes. I have made friends with people I would never have met. And I arrive at work calm instead of stressed.","My neighbor, the old man who helped me on my first day, once said something that I still remember. \"When you drive, you only see the road,\" he told me. \"When you take the bus, you see the people.\" I did not understand what he meant at that time. Now I do. Tomorrow morning, I will walk to the bus stop at the end of my street, wait for bus number 32, and say good morning to the old woman who goes to the temple. And I will smile, because I know it will be a good day."]},{"id":"08-a-birthday-party","title":"A Birthday Party","words":1582,"paragraphs":["Last month, my little brother Nam turned eleven. For weeks before his birthday, he talked about nothing else. Every evening at dinner, he asked us, \"How many days until my birthday?\" And every evening, my mother answered with a smile, \"Not long now.\" Nam wanted a big party with all his friends from school. My parents agreed, but only if he helped to plan it. So the whole family started to prepare.","The first thing we did was make a list of guests. Nam wanted to invite his whole class, which has thirty-five students. My mother laughed. \"Our house is not big enough for thirty-five children!\" she said. \"You can invite ten friends.\" Nam thought about it for a long time. It was very difficult for him to choose. In the end, he chose eight friends from his class and two boys who live on our street. He also wanted to invite our cousins, our aunt and uncle, and of course, our grandmother, who lives with us.","Next, Nam made the invitations himself. He did not want to buy cards from a shop. He wanted to draw them. He spent a whole Saturday afternoon at the kitchen table with colored pencils, paper, and stickers. On each card, he drew a big cake with eleven candles, some balloons, and a picture of a football, because football is his favorite sport. Inside, he wrote: \"Please come to my birthday party! Saturday, at three o'clock, at my house. There will be cake, games, and lots of fun!\" He wrote each friend's name very carefully on the front of the card. Some of the letters were not very straight, but the cards were beautiful. The next Monday, he took them to school and gave them to his friends.","My parents and I planned the food. Children love sweet food, but my mother also wanted to give them something healthy. So we decided to make a mix of both. For the main food, we would have small sandwiches, fried chicken, spring rolls, and fruit. For drinks, we would have orange juice, water, and milk tea. And of course, there would be a birthday cake. Nam wanted a chocolate cake with football decorations on top. My aunt is very good at baking, so she offered to make it as her birthday gift to him.","On the morning of the party, the whole house was busy. My father and I cleaned the living room and moved the sofa and the table against the walls to make space for games. My mother and my grandmother cooked in the kitchen. The house smelled wonderful: fried chicken, warm bread, and fresh fruit. Nam was so excited that he could not stay still. He ran from room to room, asking, \"Can I help? Can I help?\" Finally, my mother gave him a job. She asked him to blow up thirty balloons. That kept him busy for a long time. By the time he finished, his face was red, and he was out of breath, but he was very proud.","We hung the balloons from the ceiling and on the walls. We also put up a big sign that said \"Happy Birthday, Nam!\" in bright colors. My father put a colorful paper cloth on the table and arranged the plates, cups, and napkins. My grandmother made a small bunch of flowers from her garden and put it in the middle of the table. When everything was ready, the room looked like a different place. It was bright, colorful, and full of happy energy.","At a quarter to three, my aunt arrived with the cake. It was even better than we had imagined. It was a big, round chocolate cake with green cream on top that looked like a football field. In the middle, there were two small plastic football players and a tiny ball. On the side, she had written \"Happy Eleventh Birthday, Nam\" in white cream. When Nam saw it, his mouth fell open. \"Wow!\" he shouted. \"It's the best cake in the world!\" He hugged our aunt so hard that she almost dropped her bag.","The guests began to arrive at three o'clock. Nam stood by the front door and welcomed each friend with a big smile. Every friend brought a present wrapped in colorful paper. Nam put all the presents on a small table in the corner. He wanted to open them right away, but my mother told him to wait until after the cake. \"Patience is a good thing to learn,\" she said. Nam did not look very happy about that, but he agreed.","When all the children had arrived, my father organized some games. The first game was musical chairs. We put nine chairs in a circle, one less than the number of children. My father played music on his phone, and the children walked around the chairs. When the music stopped, everybody had to find a chair and sit down quickly. The child without a chair was out. Then we took away one more chair and started again. The children laughed and screamed and pushed each other. In the end, a small, quiet girl called Linh won. Everyone was surprised, because she was the smallest child in the group, but she was also the fastest.","The second game was a treasure hunt. Before the party, my father had hidden ten small bags of candy in different places in the house and on the balcony. He gave the children some clues on small pieces of paper. For example, one clue said, \"I am cold inside, and I keep your food fresh.\" The children thought for a moment, and then one boy shouted, \"The fridge!\" They all ran to the kitchen, and there was a bag of candy on top of the fridge. Another clue said, \"You sit on me to watch television.\" That was the sofa. The children loved this game. They ran all over the house, shouting and laughing, until they found every bag.","After the games, everyone was hungry. The children sat around the table and ate sandwiches, chicken, and spring rolls. They drank a lot of juice. My grandmother walked around the table and put more food on everyone's plate. \"Eat, eat,\" she said. \"Growing children need a lot of food.\" The parents who came with their children sat in the kitchen with my mother and drank tea. They talked about school, homework, and how fast their children were growing.","Then it was time for the cake. My mother turned off the lights and closed the curtains. My aunt lit the eleven candles on the cake and carried it slowly into the living room. Everybody started to sing \"Happy Birthday.\" Nam sat at the end of the table, his face shining in the light of the candles. When the song finished, my mother said, \"Make a wish, Nam!\" Nam closed his eyes for a few seconds. Then he took a big breath and blew out all eleven candles at once. Everyone clapped and cheered. Later, I asked him what his wish was, but he would not tell me. \"If I tell you, it won't come true,\" he said.","My aunt cut the cake into pieces, and everyone got a slice. It was delicious: soft, sweet, and full of chocolate. Some of the children got cream on their noses and faces, and everyone laughed. One boy ate three pieces and then said his stomach hurt. His mother shook her head and said, \"I told you so.\"","Finally, it was time to open the presents. Nam sat on the floor, and his friends sat around him. He opened each present slowly and carefully. He got a new football, two books, a puzzle, a toy car, some colored pens, a shirt from his favorite football team, and a board game. Our cousins gave him a small toy robot that could walk and talk. My parents gave him a new bicycle, which they had hidden on the balcony. When Nam saw the bicycle, he jumped up and down and shouted with joy. He thanked everyone for every present, even the ones he did not like very much. My mother had taught him that it is important to be polite and grateful.","At about six o'clock, the parents came to take their children home. Each child got a small bag with some candy and a balloon to take home. Nam stood at the door and said goodbye to each friend. \"Thank you for coming!\" he said again and again. When the last guest had left, the house was suddenly very quiet. There were pieces of wrapping paper all over the floor, half-empty cups on the table, and balloons in every corner. It was a big mess, but nobody cared.","That night, Nam was very tired, but very happy. Before he went to bed, he came to my room and sat on my bed. \"That was the best birthday ever,\" he said. \"Thank you for helping.\" I hugged him and said, \"Happy birthday, little brother.\" Then he went to his room and fell asleep in less than five minutes, holding his new toy robot in his arms. I sat in my room for a while and thought about my own birthdays when I was a child. I realized that the presents were never the most important part. The most important part was the people: my family, my friends, the laughter, the songs, and the feeling that I was loved."]}];
